+# PowerTiger
+
+`build-stack.py` builds the pinned Octotiger dependency stack using the Spack
+installation provided by the build machine. It is the maintained entry point;
+the older `build-*.sh` scripts describe a historical GCC 8/C++14 environment and
+are not invoked by this driver.
+
+## Building the locked stack
+
+Provide an Octotiger checkout and its `.jenkins/lsu/hpx2rc` bundle:
+
+```sh
+python3 build-stack.py \
+  --stack-dir /absolute/path/octotiger/.jenkins/lsu/hpx2rc \
+  --source /absolute/path/octotiger \
+  --spec 'octotiger@develop %gcc@13.2.0 +kokkos ~cuda ~rocm ~sycl' \
+  --jobs 4
+```
+
+Use the complete Jenkins matrix spec to select its existing backend and test
+parameters. `--dirty` forwards Spack's dirty-environment option when a site
+requires it. Compiler and floating-point flags from the legacy shell scripts
+are cleared in either mode; module paths and GPU visibility settings remain.
+The job count must fit the current CPU affinity and Slurm CPUs per task. Outside
+Slurm, the driver also caps a run at half the machine's logical CPUs; callers
+must still reserve that budget across simultaneous local work.
+
+The driver verifies the bundle's patch hashes and core recipe commit pins,
+then stages recipes and adjacent patches in
+`SOURCE/.jenkins-spack-overlay/repo`. A private configuration directory contains
+repository precedence, compiler registrations, the job count, and an isolated
+Spack metadata cache. It preserves site configuration for supporting packages,
+but disallows external HPX, Kokkos, HPXKokkos, CPPuddle, and Silo. The manifest records
+exact recipe hashes, source pins, selected compiler, and requested spec.
+Nothing is written to the site's compiler configuration. Kokkos has no source
+patches in this stack. HPX applies its locked stdexec patches internally.
+
+## GCC bootstrap
+
+For `%gcc@13.2.0` or SYCL specs, existing configured compiler paths are checked
+for an executable GCC/G++ 13.2.0 pair, checked with a bounded C++20
+`<concepts>`/`<span>` syntax probe, and registered with the operating system
+reported on the current node. This permits a valid compiler previously
+registered under an older OS name to be used without editing central settings.
+Clang-only specs do not force a GCC bootstrap.
+
+If needed, Spack builds `gcc@13.2.0 languages=c,c++ target=x86_64 %gcc@11` from
+source with the same bounded job count. A runnable GCC11 pair is required;
+Fortran is not built. A shared file lock serializes that bootstrap, with a
+maximum lock wait of 120 minutes. The default coordination directory is
+`SOURCE/../.powertiger-bootstrap`; set `POWERTIGER_BOOTSTRAP_DIR` to a shared
+absolute path when matrix checkouts do not share a parent directory. The
+compiler itself lives in Spack's existing installation tree. The bootstrap
+record stores only verified compiler paths; each node checks them again.
+
+Finally, the driver runs a fresh Spack concretization and `dev-build` through
+the build phase with root-package tests enabled and verbose output. Test
+failures propagate. The Octotiger recipe controls the full CTest suite and its
+per-test timeout; this driver does not remove tests or adjust tolerances.
+
+The checked-in lock describes successful local GCC13/Clang21/CUDA12.8 results.
+Other compilers, architectures, and HIP/SYCL configurations require their own
+Jenkins validation. Staging and mocked-driver checks do not establish build or
+runtime success.
